<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0

    CPack WiX patch fragment: registers fastcached as an auto-start Windows
    service at install time and removes it on uninstall.

    Rather than declaring a WiX ServiceInstall element, this drives the
    daemon's own install-service / uninstall-service code path
    (FastCache/Platform/ServiceControl.cpp). That keeps one implementation of
    "how fastcached registers itself" shared with the command line, including
    BuildServiceCommandLine's absolutization of the config path, which matters
    because a service starts with its working directory set to
    C:\Windows\System32, so a relative path captured at install time would
    resolve somewhere else entirely at boot.

    NOTE: XML forbids a double hyphen inside a comment, so the flag names are
    spelled without their leading dashes here. CPack parses this file with a
    strict reader that rejects the file outright otherwise.

    Registration deliberately leaves the new service STOPPED, so starting it
    is a separate action, conditioned on FASTCACHED_START_SERVICE so the user
    can opt out in the installer UI.

    All actions are deferred with Impersonate="no": creating a service needs
    the elevated context, which is only available to a deferred action running
    as the install account.
-->
<CPackWiXPatch>

  <!-- INSTALL_ROOT is what CPack's WiX generator names the install root
       directory. It is NOT the WiX-default INSTALLFOLDER: referencing that
       name yields an unrelated directory built from the vendor and product
       ("C:\Program Files\LASTRADA Software fastcached\") rather than the
       "C:\Program Files\fastcached\" the files actually land in, so the
       action ran in a folder with no fastcached.exe in it and failed the
       install with error 1721. -->
  <CPackWiXFragment Id="#PRODUCT">

    <!-- Checkbox state, surfaced in the UI (see the exit dialog below).
         1 = start the service when the installer finishes. -->
    <Property Id="FASTCACHED_START_SERVICE" Value="1" Secure="yes" />

    <!-- Create the machine-wide config directory, and give it an ACL of its
         own, BEFORE anything writes into it.

         This is not a nicety. C:\ProgramData grants BUILTIN\Users
         (CI)(WD,AD,WEA,WA) — create files and folders — and that ACE is
         inherited by every subdirectory. A directory left to inherit it lets
         any standard user drop a fastcached.yaml into it, and that file
         configures a service running as LocalSystem: storage_path alone makes
         SYSTEM create directories and write files at a path the planter chose.
         The previous machine-wide location was under C:\Program Files, where
         only administrators can create files, so the exposure arrived with the
         move to %ProgramData% and is closed here.

         PermissionEx/@Sddl, not Permission/@User and not util:PermissionEx.
         Permission and util:PermissionEx both take an account NAME, which is
         resolved at install time and is therefore wrong on a non-English
         Windows; util:PermissionEx additionally needs an extension package and
         has no Sddl attribute at all (WIX0004 if you try). PermissionEx is
         built in, writes MsiLockPermissionsEx (Windows Installer 5.0, i.e.
         Windows 7 and later), and takes raw SDDL: SY / BA / BU are S-1-5-18,
         S-1-5-32-544 and S-1-5-32-545, the same on every localisation. D:P
         breaks inheritance — the entire point — and 0x1200a9 is
         FILE_GENERIC_READ|FILE_GENERIC_EXECUTE.

         CreateFolder runs in the CreateFolders standard action, which is
         sequenced before InstallFiles and therefore before the seeding action
         below: the directory is already locked down when the config lands in it.

         The Guid is explicit because WiX refuses an automatic one for a
         component whose keypath is a directory rather than a file. Minted once
         for this component; NEVER regenerate it, for the reason spelled out at
         CPACK_WIX_UPGRADE_GUID in cmake/Packaging.cmake.

         Permanent="yes" because uninstalling deliberately leaves the
         operator's config in place: a directory removed out from under a file
         that survives it, or an ACL handed back to inheritance while the file
         is still there, would both be worse than never having secured it. -->
    <StandardDirectory Id="CommonAppDataFolder">
      <Directory Id="FastCachedProgramData" Name="fastcached">
        <Component Id="FastCachedProgramDataDir"
                   Guid="84BE18BD-514D-4D51-B22C-5BCF34255AE0"
                   Permanent="yes">
          <CreateFolder>
            <PermissionEx Sddl="D:P(A;OICI;FA;;;SY)(A;OICI;FA;;;BA)(A;OICI;0x1200a9;;;BU)" />
          </CreateFolder>
        </Component>
      </Directory>
    </StandardDirectory>

    <!-- Put a default configuration at the machine-wide location the daemon
         looks in when it is started with no config flag, but only when nothing
         is there yet, so an upgrade never discards operator edits. The payload
         under [INSTALL_ROOT] is only the replaceable template; the live file
         lives in %ProgramData% and is deliberately not payload at all.

         The daemon does the copying itself rather than a cmd.exe copy, so the
         destination is decided by the same table the startup lookup walks and
         the two cannot drift apart. -->
    <CustomAction Id="FastCachedSeedConfig"
                  Directory="INSTALL_ROOT"
                  ExeCommand="&quot;[INSTALL_ROOT]bin\fastcached.exe&quot; --seed-config=&quot;[INSTALL_ROOT]etc\fastcached.yaml.default&quot;"
                  Execute="deferred"
                  Impersonate="no"
                  Return="ignore" />

    <!-- Directory + ExeCommand is a type 34 custom action, whose Target must be
         the FULL path to the executable, quoted when it contains spaces (it
         does: "C:\Program Files\fastcached\"). Directory= is NOT a working
         directory that makes a relative command resolve; a relative Target
         simply cannot be launched, which is error 1721 ("a program required
         for this install to complete could not be run").
         See https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/msi/custom-action-type-34

         [INSTALL_ROOT] is safe here even though the action is deferred: the
         Target of a type 34 action is formatted when the action is SCHEDULED,
         in the immediate sequence, where properties are still available. It is
         the deferred action's own session that cannot read properties, which
         is a different thing.

         No config flag is passed on purpose. A path baked into the service's
         ImagePath outranks the file itself for the life of the registration,
         and it would turn a seed that did not happen into a service that fails
         at every start. Registering nothing lets the daemon resolve
         %ProgramData%\fastcached\fastcached.yaml at each start, and fall back to
         its built-in defaults if that file is genuinely absent.

         Return="ignore": registering the service must never fail the whole
         installation. It legitimately returns non-zero when the service is
         already present (a repair, or a reinstall over a previous version),
         and a machine with the files installed but the service unregistered is
         far more recoverable than one rolled back to nothing: the operator can
         simply re-run the install-service flag by hand. -->
    <CustomAction Id="FastCachedInstallService"
                  Directory="INSTALL_ROOT"
                  ExeCommand="&quot;[INSTALL_ROOT]bin\fastcached.exe&quot; --install-service"
                  Execute="deferred"
                  Impersonate="no"
                  Return="ignore" />

    <CustomAction Id="FastCachedStartService"
                  Directory="INSTALL_ROOT"
                  ExeCommand="&quot;[System64Folder]sc.exe&quot; start FastCached"
                  Execute="deferred"
                  Impersonate="no"
                  Return="ignore" />

    <!-- Uninstall must not fail the removal if the service is already gone,
         hence Return="ignore". It best-effort stops before deleting. -->
    <CustomAction Id="FastCachedUninstallService"
                  Directory="INSTALL_ROOT"
                  ExeCommand="&quot;[INSTALL_ROOT]bin\fastcached.exe&quot; --uninstall-service"
                  Execute="deferred"
                  Impersonate="no"
                  Return="ignore" />

    <!-- The COMPILE WORKER, which this MSI previously installed as a binary and
         never registered: the node refused to install without a toolchain named,
         and an installer cannot know what a machine holds.

         It can now. The worker surveys the machine at startup and serves what it
         finds, so the per-machine half of the configuration is gone and the MSI
         has a registration it can actually write.

         What is left is genuinely site-specific and the installer still cannot
         guess it: WHERE the scheduler is, and WHAT ADDRESS clients should use to
         reach this worker. Neither has a defensible default. The advertise flag in
         particular falls back to 0.0.0.0, which no client can dial, and such a
         worker registers, heartbeats, is leased out and is never reached, with no
         error at either end. So they are installer properties, and with neither
         set nothing is registered at all:

             msiexec /i fastcached.msi ^
                 FASTCACHE_NODE_SCHEDULER=build-cache.internal:6675 ^
                 FASTCACHE_NODE_ADVERTISE=worker-01.internal:6676

         NOTE, as at the top of this file: XML forbids a double hyphen inside a
         comment, so flag names are written without their leading dashes here.

         Secure="yes" so the values survive the elevation to the deferred action's
         own session, exactly as FASTCACHED_START_SERVICE does. -->
    <Property Id="FASTCACHE_NODE_SCHEDULER" Secure="yes" />
    <Property Id="FASTCACHE_NODE_ADVERTISE" Secure="yes" />

    <CustomAction Id="FastCacheNodeInstallService"
                  Directory="INSTALL_ROOT"
                  ExeCommand="&quot;[INSTALL_ROOT]bin\fastcache-compile-node.exe&quot; --install-service --scheduler=[FASTCACHE_NODE_SCHEDULER] --advertise=[FASTCACHE_NODE_ADVERTISE]"
                  Execute="deferred"
                  Impersonate="no"
                  Return="ignore" />

    <CustomAction Id="FastCacheNodeUninstallService"
                  Directory="INSTALL_ROOT"
                  ExeCommand="&quot;[INSTALL_ROOT]bin\fastcache-compile-node.exe&quot; --uninstall-service"
                  Execute="deferred"
                  Impersonate="no"
                  Return="ignore" />

    <InstallExecuteSequence>
      <!-- Seed before registering, so the service finds its configuration the
           first time it starts. Both run after the files are on disk
           (InstallFiles), since they execute the installed binary. -->
      <Custom Action="FastCachedSeedConfig" After="InstallFiles"
              Condition="NOT REMOVE" />

      <!-- Register after the files are on disk (InstallFiles), since the
           action executes the installed binary. NOT REMOVE keeps it off the
           uninstall path; upgrades re-register with a refreshed command line. -->
      <Custom Action="FastCachedInstallService" After="FastCachedSeedConfig"
              Condition="NOT REMOVE" />

      <Custom Action="FastCachedStartService" After="FastCachedInstallService"
              Condition="NOT REMOVE AND FASTCACHED_START_SERVICE = &quot;1&quot;" />

      <!-- The worker, registered only when the installer was told both of the two
           things it cannot guess. Conditioned on BOTH rather than either: a
           registration with a scheduler and no advertised endpoint bakes in
           0.0.0.0, and that worker is leased out and never reached, which is the
           worst shape this system has because it looks healthy from both ends.

           A property that was not passed formats as the empty string, so the
           condition tests for a non-empty value rather than for the property's
           existence. -->
      <Custom Action="FastCacheNodeInstallService" After="FastCachedStartService"
              Condition="NOT REMOVE AND FASTCACHE_NODE_SCHEDULER AND FASTCACHE_NODE_ADVERTISE" />

      <!-- Deregister before the files are removed: the action needs
           fastcached.exe to still exist. -->
      <Custom Action="FastCachedUninstallService" Before="RemoveFiles"
              Condition="REMOVE~=&quot;ALL&quot;" />

      <!-- Unconditional on the way out, unlike the install: an uninstall merely
           names a registration to remove, and refusing to remove one because the
           properties were not passed a second time is how a stale registration
           becomes permanent. Return="ignore" already covers the case where there
           was none. -->
      <Custom Action="FastCacheNodeUninstallService" Before="FastCachedUninstallService"
              Condition="REMOVE~=&quot;ALL&quot;" />
    </InstallExecuteSequence>

  </CPackWiXFragment>

  <!-- A component reaches the install only through a feature. #PRODUCTFEATURE
       is CPack's name for the root one, which every other component of this
       package already belongs to. -->
  <CPackWiXFragment Id="#PRODUCTFEATURE">
    <ComponentRef Id="FastCachedProgramDataDir" />
  </CPackWiXFragment>

  <!-- Put the install's bin directory on the system PATH.

       Without this the MSI is the only one of the three platform packages that
       leaves the executables unreachable by name: the .deb/.rpm land in
       /usr/local/bin and the .pkg installs /etc/paths.d/fastcached, both already
       on PATH, while a Windows install of fastcache-cc stays invisible to every
       build system that looks it up as a bare command name.

       The Environment element must live inside a Component, so this patches the
       component CPack generates for bin\fastcached.exe rather than #PRODUCT.
       Tying the entry to a component makes removal automatic: it is installed
       and uninstalled with the file, needing no custom action.

       CPack builds that Id as CM_CP_<cpack component>.<install path>, so the
       "Runtime" segment is the CPack component these files install under and
       NOT a directory. Characters WiX forbids in an Id become underscores,
       which is why the sibling component is CM_CP_Runtime.bin.fastcache_cc.exe
       with a hyphen-free spelling. A mismatched Id is caught rather than
       silently ignored: CPack fails with "Some XML patch fragments did not have
       matching IDs". The authoritative list is the generated
       _CPack_Packages/win64/WIX/files.wxs.

       Part="last" appends rather than replaces. Permanent="no" is what lets the
       uninstall take the entry back out again. System="yes" writes the machine
       PATH, which this per-machine MSI is already elevated for.

       Note this only affects processes started AFTER the install; an already
       open shell keeps the environment block it was launched with. -->
  <CPackWiXFragment Id="CM_CP_Runtime.bin.fastcached.exe">
    <Environment Id="FastCachedBinOnPath"
                 Name="PATH"
                 Value="[INSTALL_ROOT]bin"
                 Action="set"
                 Part="last"
                 Permanent="no"
                 System="yes" />
  </CPackWiXFragment>

</CPackWiXPatch>
